1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,718
Two bills came to one house.

2
00:00:02,018 --> 00:00:03,876
Pay or face termination.

3
00:00:04,176 --> 00:00:08,123
Mary Craft telephoned after a final notice and said she had paid a bill.

4
00:00:09,923 --> 00:00:13,545
The case is Memphis Light, Gas and Water Division against Craft.

5
00:00:13,845 --> 00:00:18,582
Argued in November 1977, decided on the first of May, 1978.

6
00:00:18,882 --> 00:00:20,879
Justice Powell wrote for the Court.

7
00:00:21,179 --> 00:00:26,067
The Supreme Court affirmed the judgment below and sent the damages question back

8
00:00:26,067 --> 00:00:26,984
down undecided.

9
00:00:27,284 --> 00:00:31,605
Three Justices dissented, and their account of the same record is printed in the

10
00:00:31,605 --> 00:00:32,253
same volume.

11
00:00:34,053 --> 00:00:39,208
Willie S. and Mary Craft, respondents here, reside on Alaska Street in Memphis.

12
00:00:39,508 --> 00:00:43,734
Their names and that street are very nearly everything the Court says about them.

13
00:00:44,034 --> 00:00:45,009
No ages.

14
00:00:45,309 --> 00:00:46,563
No occupations.

15
00:00:46,863 --> 00:00:47,931
No children.

16
00:00:48,231 --> 00:00:53,174
When the Crafts moved into their residence in October 1972, they noticed that there

17
00:00:53,174 --> 00:00:57,819
were two separate gas and electric meters and only one water meter serving the

18
00:00:57,819 --> 00:00:58,355
premises.

19
00:00:58,655 --> 00:01:01,441
The residence had been used previously as a duplex.

20
00:01:01,741 --> 00:01:06,351
The Crafts assumed, on the basis of information from the seller, that the second set

21
00:01:06,351 --> 00:01:07,778
of meters was inoperative.

22
00:01:08,078 --> 00:01:12,783
Then, in 1973, the Crafts began receiving two bills: their regular bill, and a

23
00:01:12,783 --> 00:01:17,608
second bill with an account number in the name of Willie C. Craft, as opposed to

24
00:01:17,608 --> 00:01:18,573
Willie S. Craft.

25
00:01:18,873 --> 00:01:23,285
One house, one family, two customers as far as the billing system was concerned.

26
00:01:23,585 --> 00:01:28,142
Separate monthly bills were received for each set of meters, with a city service fee

27
00:01:28,142 --> 00:01:29,390
appearing on each bill.

28
00:01:29,690 --> 00:01:32,476
Municipal ordinance required that fee as a separate item.

29
00:01:32,776 --> 00:01:34,541
October 1973.

30
00:01:34,841 --> 00:01:39,868
After learning from a MLG&W meter reader that both sets of meters were running in

31
00:01:39,868 --> 00:01:45,020
their home, the Crafts hired a private plumber and electrical contractor to combine

32
00:01:45,020 --> 00:01:47,937
the meters into one gas and one electric meter.

33
00:01:48,237 --> 00:01:49,816
They paid for that themselves.

34
00:01:50,116 --> 00:01:53,181
And then the sentence the first half of this case turns on.

35
00:01:53,481 --> 00:01:58,627
Because the contractor did not consolidate the meters properly, a condition of which

36
00:01:58,627 --> 00:02:03,651
the Crafts were not aware, they continued to receive two bills until January 1974.

37
00:02:03,951 --> 00:02:06,087
That is the majority's account.

38
00:02:06,387 --> 00:02:09,824
There is a second account of the same events, inside the same opinion.

39
00:02:10,124 --> 00:02:14,489
In a footnote, the Court quotes the brief filed by the Crafts' own lawyers.

40
00:02:14,789 --> 00:02:19,607
Not until after the action was filed were the Crafts able to discover that they

41
00:02:19,607 --> 00:02:24,547
continued to receive double computer billings because MLG&W failed to combine the

42
00:02:24,547 --> 00:02:25,888
two accounts properly.

43
00:02:26,188 --> 00:02:29,021
That is the Crafts' account, and the Court printed it.

44
00:02:29,321 --> 00:02:30,668
Whose mistake was it?

45
00:02:30,968 --> 00:02:32,501
The body says the meters.

46
00:02:32,801 --> 00:02:34,798
The footnote says the accounts.

47
00:02:35,098 --> 00:02:38,767
The record does not agree with itself, and the Court never resolves it.

48
00:02:39,067 --> 00:02:41,343
That shape returns twice more.

49
00:02:41,643 --> 00:02:45,625
During this period, the Crafts' utility service was terminated five times for

50
00:02:45,625 --> 00:02:46,194
nonpayment.

51
00:02:46,494 --> 00:02:48,073
During this period.

52
00:02:48,373 --> 00:02:49,998
That phrase is the Court's.

53
00:02:50,298 --> 00:02:52,713
No month for any of the majority's five.

54
00:02:53,013 --> 00:02:54,174
No duration.

55
00:02:54,474 --> 00:02:55,542
No season.

56
00:02:55,842 --> 00:02:57,746
No figure for any bill behind them.

57
00:02:58,046 --> 00:03:02,920
On several occasions, Mrs. Craft missed work and went to the MLG&W offices in order

58
00:03:02,920 --> 00:03:05,151
to resolve the double billing problem.

59
00:03:05,451 --> 00:03:06,473
Missed work.

60
00:03:06,773 --> 00:03:11,755
As found by the District Court, Mrs. Craft sought in good faith to determine the

61
00:03:11,755 --> 00:03:16,924
cause of the double billing, but was unable to obtain a satisfactory explanation or

62
00:03:16,924 --> 00:03:20,473
any suggestion for further recourse from MLG&W employees.

63
00:03:20,773 --> 00:03:22,584
Good faith there is a legal term.

64
00:03:22,884 --> 00:03:25,160
It was a finding, not a compliment.

65
00:03:25,460 --> 00:03:28,479
Then the trial judge wrote down one occasion in particular.

66
00:03:28,779 --> 00:03:33,660
On one occasion when Mrs. Craft was attempting to avert a utilities termination,

67
00:03:33,660 --> 00:03:38,602
after final notice, she called the defendant's offices and explained that she had

68
00:03:38,602 --> 00:03:41,225
paid a bill, but was given no satisfaction.

69
00:03:41,525 --> 00:03:45,920
The procedure for an opportunity to talk with management was not adequately

70
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:50,725
explained to Mrs. Craft, although she repeatedly tried to get some explanation for

71
00:03:50,725 --> 00:03:54,064
the problems of two bills and possible duplicate charges.

72
00:03:54,364 --> 00:03:58,033
The Court kept what an employee actually said to her in a footnote.

73
00:03:58,333 --> 00:04:02,838
An employee of uncertain authority told Mrs. Craft, apparently without explanation

74
00:04:02,838 --> 00:04:06,739
or attempt at investigation, "well, you have to pay on the other" bill.

75
00:04:07,039 --> 00:04:08,432
Of uncertain authority.

76
00:04:08,732 --> 00:04:10,775
Apparently without explanation.

77
00:04:11,075 --> 00:04:13,304
Those qualifiers are the majority's.

78
00:04:13,604 --> 00:04:19,051
In February 1974, the Crafts and other MLG&W customers filed this action in the

79
00:04:19,051 --> 00:04:22,706
District Court for the Western District of Tennessee.

80
00:04:24,506 --> 00:04:28,964
Memphis Light, Gas and Water Division is a division of the city of Memphis which

81
00:04:28,964 --> 00:04:30,357
provides utility service.

82
00:04:30,657 --> 00:04:34,584
It is directed by a Board of Commissioners appointed by the City Council, and is

83
00:04:34,584 --> 00:04:37,530
subject to the ultimate control of the municipal government.

84
00:04:37,830 --> 00:04:40,245
The company sending the bills was the city.

85
00:04:40,545 --> 00:04:45,871
As a municipal utility, MLG&W enjoys a statutory exemption from regulation by the

86
00:04:45,871 --> 00:04:47,975
state public service commission.

87
00:04:48,275 --> 00:04:53,033
The utility argued at first that its termination policies were not government action

88
00:04:53,033 --> 00:04:53,430
at all.

89
00:04:53,730 --> 00:04:58,464
Petitioners have abandoned their contention that state action is not present in this

90
00:04:58,464 --> 00:04:58,746
case.

91
00:04:59,046 --> 00:05:04,260
The utility itself was dismissed out, because a municipality or governmental unit

92
00:05:04,260 --> 00:05:09,216
standing in that capacity is not a person within the meaning of section 1983.

93
00:05:09,516 --> 00:05:14,136
What remained were individuals, sued in both their official and personal capacities:

94
00:05:14,136 --> 00:05:18,426
the president and general manager, the vice president, members of the Board of

95
00:05:18,426 --> 00:05:23,046
Commissioners, and two employees who have had responsibility for terminating utility

96
00:05:23,046 --> 00:05:23,541
services.

97
00:05:23,841 --> 00:05:26,209
The District Court set it out in four steps.

98
00:05:26,509 --> 00:05:30,903
This is the clock the family on Alaska Street was living on, but none of the four

99
00:05:30,903 --> 00:05:32,639
says where a disputed bill goes.

100
00:05:32,939 --> 00:05:37,674
Approximately four days after a meter reading date, a bill is mailed to the service

101
00:05:37,674 --> 00:05:38,187
location.

102
00:05:38,487 --> 00:05:42,769
The last day to pay the net amount would be approximately 20 days after the meter

103
00:05:42,769 --> 00:05:43,456
reading date.

104
00:05:43,756 --> 00:05:48,988
Approximately 24 days after the meters are read, a final notice is mailed stating

105
00:05:48,988 --> 00:05:54,156
that services will be disconnected within four days if no payment is received or

106
00:05:54,156 --> 00:05:56,481
other provision for payment is made.

107
00:05:56,781 --> 00:06:01,336
Electric service is then terminated by the meter reader, unless the customer assures

108
00:06:01,336 --> 00:06:05,782
him that payment is in the mail, shows a paid receipt, or explains that nonpayment

109
00:06:05,782 --> 00:06:06,812
was due to illness.

110
00:06:07,112 --> 00:06:12,034
If there is no communication prior to termination, the meter reader or serviceman is

111
00:06:12,034 --> 00:06:16,957
instructed to leave a cutoff notice giving information about restoration of service.

112
00:06:17,257 --> 00:06:21,762
Approximately five days after the electric service cutoff, the remaining services

113
00:06:21,762 --> 00:06:26,379
are terminated if the customer has not paid the bill or made other arrangements for

114
00:06:26,379 --> 00:06:26,824
payment.

115
00:06:27,124 --> 00:06:31,738
Three things the meter reader is instructed to accept, on a doorstep, at the last

116
00:06:31,738 --> 00:06:32,650
possible minute.

117
00:06:32,950 --> 00:06:38,008
Petitioners provide for at least a 30-day period between the mailing of the bill and

118
00:06:38,008 --> 00:06:40,055
the actual termination of service.

119
00:06:40,355 --> 00:06:43,141
Thirty days of warnings, and one man at the end of them.

120
00:06:43,441 --> 00:06:48,198
Justice Stevens, dissenting, described the same operation with numbers the majority

121
00:06:48,198 --> 00:06:48,828
never uses.

122
00:06:49,128 --> 00:06:53,447
Each month the Division terminates the service of about 2,000 customers.

123
00:06:53,747 --> 00:07:00,677
During the six months from September 1973 through February 1974, there were 11,216

124
00:07:00,677 --> 00:07:03,128
so-called delinquent cutoffs.

125
00:07:03,428 --> 00:07:06,029
Then the notice, as the dissent describes it.

126
00:07:06,329 --> 00:07:08,605
The notices contain a prominent legend.

127
00:07:08,905 --> 00:07:11,738
Phone 523-0711, information center.

128
00:07:12,038 --> 00:07:16,894
Calls to the listed phone number are answered by 30 or 40 Division employees, all of

129
00:07:16,894 --> 00:07:21,692
whom are empowered to delay cutoffs for three days based on representations made by

130
00:07:21,692 --> 00:07:23,137
customers over the phone.

131
00:07:23,437 --> 00:07:28,110
These employees also direct callers to credit counselors who are authorized to

132
00:07:28,110 --> 00:07:33,142
resolve disputes on a more permanent basis and who can set up extended payment plans

133
00:07:33,142 --> 00:07:35,418
for customers in financial difficulty.

134
00:07:35,718 --> 00:07:36,972
Thirty or forty people.

135
00:07:37,272 --> 00:07:39,315
And the number was printed on the notice.

136
00:07:39,615 --> 00:07:42,076
Behind the paper there was a real procedure.

137
00:07:42,376 --> 00:07:44,141
Credit counselors came first.

138
00:07:44,441 --> 00:07:48,986
If those counselors cannot satisfy the customer, then the customer is referred to

139
00:07:48,986 --> 00:07:53,195
management personnel; generally the chief clerk in the department; then the

140
00:07:53,195 --> 00:07:55,215
supervisor in credit and collection.

141
00:07:55,515 --> 00:07:57,651
Above them sat the Board of Commissioners.

142
00:07:57,951 --> 00:08:02,641
A customer may, if he so desires, be accompanied by an appropriate representative.

143
00:08:02,941 --> 00:08:05,635
Four rungs, and the right to bring somebody with you.

144
00:08:05,935 --> 00:08:11,525
In March 1973 the utility began an extended payment plan, which the trial judge

145
00:08:11,525 --> 00:08:13,365
called a generous program.

146
00:08:13,665 --> 00:08:18,322
Customers able to demonstrate financial hardship could pay only one-half of a past

147
00:08:18,322 --> 00:08:22,581
due bill, with the balance in equal installments over the next three bills.

148
00:08:22,881 --> 00:08:26,085
The plaintiffs in this action were participants in the plan.

149
00:08:26,385 --> 00:08:30,983
Whatever else this case is, it is not a case about a utility that offered nothing.

150
00:08:31,283 --> 00:08:33,326
So what was missing?

151
00:08:33,626 --> 00:08:35,994
And this is what the notice itself said.

152
00:08:36,294 --> 00:08:41,597
The final notice contained in MLG&W's bills simply stated that payment was overdue

153
00:08:41,597 --> 00:08:46,836
and that service would be discontinued if payment was not made by a certain date.

154
00:08:47,136 --> 00:08:49,365
The Court of Appeals put it more bluntly.

155
00:08:49,665 --> 00:08:54,402
The MLG&W notice only warned the customer to pay or face termination.

156
00:08:54,702 --> 00:08:59,348
There were flyers too, folded in with the final notice, and there were two different

157
00:08:59,348 --> 00:08:59,625
ones.

158
00:08:59,925 --> 00:09:04,272
About 40 percent of the utility's customers, those living in areas serviced by

159
00:09:04,272 --> 00:09:06,612
credit counseling stations, got the first.

160
00:09:06,912 --> 00:09:10,924
If you are having difficulty paying your utility bill, bring your bill to our

161
00:09:10,924 --> 00:09:13,321
neighborhood credit counselors for assistance.

162
00:09:13,621 --> 00:09:15,897
Of that flyer the Court wrote one sentence.

163
00:09:16,197 --> 00:09:20,144
No mention was made of a procedure for the disposition of a disputed claim.

164
00:09:20,444 --> 00:09:24,299
The second flyer, sent to the remaining areas, did the other thing.

165
00:09:24,599 --> 00:09:29,194
If there is any dispute concerning the amount due, bring your bill to the office, or

166
00:09:29,194 --> 00:09:29,522
phone.

167
00:09:29,822 --> 00:09:32,516
If there is any dispute concerning the amount due.

168
00:09:32,816 --> 00:09:35,881
That is the sentence the first flyer did not contain.

169
00:09:36,181 --> 00:09:39,872
The Court of Appeals noted that there is no assurance that the Crafts were mailed

170
00:09:39,872 --> 00:09:41,011
the just mentioned flyer.

171
00:09:41,311 --> 00:09:46,330
William T. Mullen, secretary-treasurer of MLG&W, testified that the utility

172
00:09:46,330 --> 00:09:50,413
processed thirty-three thousand high bill complaints in 1973.

173
00:09:50,713 --> 00:09:55,470
He conceded, however, that no description of a dispute resolution process was ever

174
00:09:55,470 --> 00:10:00,111
distributed to the utility's customers, and there is no indication in the record

175
00:10:00,111 --> 00:10:04,636
that a written account of such a procedure was accessible to customers who had

176
00:10:04,636 --> 00:10:06,317
complaints about their bills.

177
00:10:06,617 --> 00:10:08,428
So where was the ladder written down?

178
00:10:08,728 --> 00:10:11,468
The Court finished the thought in the same footnote.

179
00:10:11,768 --> 00:10:16,465
Mrs. Craft's case reveals that the opportunity to invoke that procedure, if it

180
00:10:16,465 --> 00:10:20,499
existed at all, depended on the vagaries of word of mouth referral.

181
00:10:20,799 --> 00:10:25,816
Tennessee law did require rules regarding the adjustment of all complaints and the

182
00:10:25,816 --> 00:10:30,956
adjustment of bills to be posted or otherwise available for convenient inspection by

183
00:10:30,956 --> 00:10:35,912
customers — but only for an independent utility district, as opposed to a utility

184
00:10:35,912 --> 00:10:37,564
division of a municipality.

185
00:10:37,864 --> 00:10:40,372
The city-owned utility was the one exempt.

186
00:10:42,172 --> 00:10:46,264
After trial, the District Court refused to certify the plaintiffs' class and

187
00:10:46,264 --> 00:10:48,256
rendered judgment for the defendants.

188
00:10:48,556 --> 00:10:49,996
The Crafts lost.

189
00:10:50,296 --> 00:10:54,097
The trial court acknowledged that respondents had not been given adequate notice of

190
00:10:54,097 --> 00:10:56,937
a procedure for discussing the disputed bills with management.

191
00:10:57,237 --> 00:10:58,305
It found that.

192
00:10:58,605 --> 00:11:03,185
Then it concluded that none of the individual plaintiffs was deprived of a due

193
00:11:03,185 --> 00:11:08,058
process opportunity to be heard, nor did the circumstances indicate any substantial

194
00:11:08,058 --> 00:11:11,933
deprivation except in the possible instance of Mr. and Mrs. Craft.

195
00:11:12,233 --> 00:11:16,923
Nobody was substantially deprived — except, possibly, the Crafts.

196
00:11:17,223 --> 00:11:19,127
The judge then expressed a hope.

197
00:11:19,427 --> 00:11:22,028
His word, in quotation marks, in the opinion.

198
00:11:22,328 --> 00:11:27,333
He hoped that credit in the amount of thirty-five dollars be issued to reimburse the

199
00:11:27,333 --> 00:11:32,338
Crafts for duplicate and unnecessary charges made and expenses incurred by them with

200
00:11:32,338 --> 00:11:37,343
respect to terminations which should have been unnecessary had effectual relief been

201
00:11:37,343 --> 00:11:38,953
afforded them as requested.

202
00:11:39,253 --> 00:11:41,854
Terminations which should have been unnecessary.

203
00:11:42,154 --> 00:11:44,801
That is the trial judge, ruling for the utility.

204
00:11:45,101 --> 00:11:46,912
He recommended two changes.

205
00:11:47,212 --> 00:11:52,393
That MLG&W in the future send a certified or registered mail notice of termination

206
00:11:52,393 --> 00:11:54,921
at least four days prior to termination.

207
00:11:55,221 --> 00:11:59,795
And that such notice provide more specific information about customer service

208
00:11:59,795 --> 00:12:04,725
locations and personnel available to work out extended payment plans or adjustments

209
00:12:04,725 --> 00:12:05,438
of accounts.

210
00:12:05,738 --> 00:12:06,620
Recommended.

211
00:12:06,920 --> 00:12:08,313
Not ordered.

212
00:12:08,613 --> 00:12:13,690
In its order filed on the thirtieth of December, 1974, the court acknowledged that

213
00:12:13,690 --> 00:12:18,582
defendants had issued the recommended credit and instituted some new procedures

214
00:12:18,582 --> 00:12:23,474
which will give more definitive and adequate notice to customers of possible or

215
00:12:23,474 --> 00:12:25,331
impending cut-off of services.

216
00:12:25,631 --> 00:12:29,578
Now the money, and this is the second place the record refuses to settle.

217
00:12:29,878 --> 00:12:33,407
That credit was never a finding that anybody had been billed too much.

218
00:12:33,707 --> 00:12:39,056
The trial court's own words were that of the balance claimed by MLG&W in March,

219
00:12:39,056 --> 00:12:44,608
1974, some involved possible gas overcharges and double or duplicate billings with

220
00:12:44,608 --> 00:12:46,571
respect to city service fees.

221
00:12:46,871 --> 00:12:47,893
Possible.

222
00:12:48,193 --> 00:12:52,957
Justice Stevens, dissenting, went at the same thirty-five dollars from the other

223
00:12:52,957 --> 00:12:53,255
side.

224
00:12:53,555 --> 00:12:58,145
The trial judge evidently accepted the Division's claim that it was engaged in split

225
00:12:58,145 --> 00:13:00,057
billing rather than double billing.

226
00:13:00,357 --> 00:13:04,372
But the amounts challenged by the Crafts as the result of double billing were

227
00:13:04,372 --> 00:13:06,719
considerably larger than thirty-five dollars.

228
00:13:07,019 --> 00:13:11,695
The reference to duplicate charges apparently concerns the two dollars and fifty

229
00:13:11,695 --> 00:13:16,313
cents per month city service fee which was charged on each set of meters in the

230
00:13:16,313 --> 00:13:18,768
duplex until after they were consolidated.

231
00:13:19,068 --> 00:13:20,879
Two dollars and fifty cents a month.

232
00:13:21,179 --> 00:13:24,290
Nobody ever decided whether the Crafts owed the money.

233
00:13:24,590 --> 00:13:25,890
Not the trial court.

234
00:13:26,190 --> 00:13:27,630
Not the court of appeals.

235
00:13:27,930 --> 00:13:29,277
Not the Supreme Court.

236
00:13:29,877 --> 00:13:31,595
The Court said so in terms.

237
00:13:31,895 --> 00:13:36,473
The validity of the damages claim is a matter for initial determination by the

238
00:13:36,473 --> 00:13:37,236
courts below.

239
00:13:37,536 --> 00:13:41,677
On appeal, the Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit affirmed the District Court's

240
00:13:41,677 --> 00:13:45,618
refusal to certify a class action, but held that the procedures accorded to the

241
00:13:45,618 --> 00:13:47,613
Crafts did not comport with due process.

242
00:13:47,913 --> 00:13:49,306
The class stayed dead.

243
00:13:49,606 --> 00:13:53,507
Everything after it is a judgment about two people at one address.

244
00:13:53,807 --> 00:13:56,222
The Sixth Circuit's ruling had two limbs.

245
00:13:56,522 --> 00:14:01,262
First, notice informing the customer not only of the possibility of termination but

246
00:14:01,262 --> 00:14:04,231
also of a procedure for challenging a disputed bill.

247
00:14:04,531 --> 00:14:09,202
Second, an established procedure for resolution of disputes, or some specified

248
00:14:09,202 --> 00:14:13,633
avenue of relief for customers who dispute the existence of the liability.

249
00:14:13,933 --> 00:14:18,066
Certiorari was granted on the twenty-second of February, 1977.

250
00:14:18,366 --> 00:14:22,267
And then, before any of it could be decided, the case nearly evaporated.

251
00:14:22,567 --> 00:14:24,378
The Court records only this.

252
00:14:24,678 --> 00:14:28,868
Respondents no longer desire a hearing to resolve a continuing dispute over their

253
00:14:28,868 --> 00:14:32,851
bills, as the double-meter problem has been clarified during this litigation.

254
00:14:33,151 --> 00:14:37,656
Nor do respondents aver that there is a present threat of termination of service.

255
00:14:37,956 --> 00:14:39,442
The Court put it plainly.

256
00:14:39,742 --> 00:14:45,124
Respondents' claim for actual and punitive damages arising from MLG&W's terminations

257
00:14:45,124 --> 00:14:48,519
of service saves this cause from the bar of mootness.

258
00:14:50,319 --> 00:14:55,056
Does a city utility customer hold anything the Constitution recognises as property?

259
00:14:55,356 --> 00:14:57,167
That is where the Court starts.

260
00:14:57,467 --> 00:14:59,464
State law creates the interest.

261
00:14:59,764 --> 00:15:03,975
Federal constitutional law decides whether it rises to a legitimate claim of

262
00:15:03,975 --> 00:15:04,640
entitlement.

263
00:15:04,940 --> 00:15:08,237
So the answer was in Tennessee, and the Court went and got it.

264
00:15:08,537 --> 00:15:13,244
A company supplying electricity to the public has a right to cut off service to a

265
00:15:13,244 --> 00:15:18,009
customer for nonpayment of a just service bill and the company may adopt a rule to

266
00:15:18,009 --> 00:15:18,707
that effect.

267
00:15:19,007 --> 00:15:23,239
An exception to the general rule exists when the customer has a bona fide dispute

268
00:15:23,239 --> 00:15:25,276
concerning the correctness of the bill.

269
00:15:25,576 --> 00:15:28,734
The same decision put the risk of error on the utility.

270
00:15:29,034 --> 00:15:34,007
If the public utility discontinues service for nonpayment of a disputed amount it

271
00:15:34,007 --> 00:15:38,673
does so at its peril and if the public utility was wrong, for example if the

272
00:15:38,673 --> 00:15:41,805
customer was overcharged, it is liable for damages.

273
00:15:42,105 --> 00:15:44,891
It refused to let a rulebook override that.

274
00:15:45,191 --> 00:15:49,928
A public utility should not be able to coerce a customer to pay a disputed claim.

275
00:15:50,228 --> 00:15:53,757
State law does not permit a public utility to terminate service at will.

276
00:15:54,057 --> 00:15:58,733
A Tennessee utility is obliged to serve all of the inhabitants of the city of its

277
00:15:58,733 --> 00:16:03,293
location alike, without discrimination, and without denial, except for good and

278
00:16:03,293 --> 00:16:04,274
sufficient cause.

279
00:16:04,574 --> 00:16:09,113
Because petitioners may terminate service only for cause, respondents assert a

280
00:16:09,113 --> 00:16:13,769
legitimate claim of entitlement within the protection of the Due Process Clause.

281
00:16:14,069 --> 00:16:18,734
The customer's right to continued service is conditioned upon payment of the charges

282
00:16:18,734 --> 00:16:19,456
properly due.

283
00:16:19,756 --> 00:16:24,306
But the Fourteenth Amendment's protection of property has never been interpreted to

284
00:16:24,306 --> 00:16:27,047
safeguard only the rights of undisputed ownership.

285
00:16:27,347 --> 00:16:28,415
Notice first.

286
00:16:28,715 --> 00:16:33,352
The standard comes from a 1950 dispute about telling the beneficiaries of a trust

287
00:16:33,352 --> 00:16:33,638
fund.

288
00:16:33,938 --> 00:16:38,925
An elementary and fundamental requirement of due process in any proceeding which is

289
00:16:38,925 --> 00:16:43,131
to be accorded finality is notice reasonably calculated, under all the

290
00:16:43,131 --> 00:16:47,817
circumstances, to apprise interested parties of the pendency of the action and

291
00:16:47,817 --> 00:16:51,121
afford them an opportunity to present their objections.

292
00:16:51,421 --> 00:16:52,489
Two halves.

293
00:16:52,789 --> 00:16:54,972
Tell them the thing is happening.

294
00:16:55,272 --> 00:16:56,990
And tell them they can object to it.

295
00:16:57,290 --> 00:16:59,984
The Memphis notice did the first and not the second.

296
00:17:00,284 --> 00:17:04,823
Petitioners' notification procedure, while adequate to apprise the Crafts of the

297
00:17:04,823 --> 00:17:09,419
threat of termination of service, was not reasonably calculated to inform them of

298
00:17:09,419 --> 00:17:13,844
the availability of an opportunity to present their objections to their bills.

299
00:17:14,144 --> 00:17:15,305
Then the holding.

300
00:17:15,605 --> 00:17:20,490
Notice in a case of this kind does not comport with constitutional requirements when

301
00:17:20,490 --> 00:17:25,317
it does not advise the customer of the availability of a procedure for protesting a

302
00:17:25,317 --> 00:17:28,515
proposed termination of utility service as unjustified.

303
00:17:28,815 --> 00:17:33,385
As no such notice was given respondents, despite good faith efforts on their part,

304
00:17:33,385 --> 00:17:36,338
they were deprived of the notice which was their due.

305
00:17:36,638 --> 00:17:39,610
And the Court said what a sufficient notice would contain.

306
00:17:39,910 --> 00:17:44,640
In essence, recipients of a cutoff notice should be told where, during which hours

307
00:17:44,640 --> 00:17:48,966
of the day, and before whom disputed bills appropriately may be considered.

308
00:17:49,266 --> 00:17:50,659
Where.

309
00:17:50,959 --> 00:17:51,888
What hours.

310
00:17:52,188 --> 00:17:53,256
Before whom.

311
00:17:53,556 --> 00:17:56,528
Three items on paper the utility was already mailing.

312
00:17:56,828 --> 00:17:58,407
Second, the hearing.

313
00:17:58,707 --> 00:18:02,774
This Court consistently has held that some kind of hearing is required at some time

314
00:18:02,774 --> 00:18:05,812
before a person is finally deprived of his property interests.

315
00:18:06,112 --> 00:18:10,801
We agree with the Court of Appeals that due process requires the provision of an

316
00:18:10,801 --> 00:18:15,665
opportunity for the presentation to a designated employee of a customer's complaint

317
00:18:15,665 --> 00:18:19,533
that he is being overcharged or charged for services not rendered.

318
00:18:19,833 --> 00:18:21,458
A designated employee.

319
00:18:21,758 --> 00:18:25,241
Not a judge, not a hearing officer, not a lawyer.

320
00:18:25,541 --> 00:18:28,002
The Court said in a footnote who it meant.

321
00:18:28,302 --> 00:18:32,691
Designated personnel who were duly authorized to review disputed bills with

322
00:18:32,691 --> 00:18:35,500
complaining customers and to correct any errors.

323
00:18:35,800 --> 00:18:40,828
Whether or not such a procedure may be available to other MLG&W customers, both

324
00:18:40,828 --> 00:18:44,902
courts below found that it was not made available to Mrs. Craft.

325
00:18:45,202 --> 00:18:49,761
That was a concurrent finding of fact by two courts, and the utility had not made

326
00:18:49,761 --> 00:18:51,843
the showing required to overturn one.

327
00:18:52,143 --> 00:18:55,254
Then the balance, borrowed from a case about disability benefits.

328
00:18:55,554 --> 00:18:57,690
On the private interest, one sentence.

329
00:18:57,990 --> 00:19:02,977
Utility service is a necessity of modern life; indeed, the discontinuance of water

330
00:19:02,977 --> 00:19:07,417
or heating for even short periods of time may threaten health and safety.

331
00:19:07,717 --> 00:19:12,895
On the risk: the risk of an erroneous deprivation, given the necessary reliance on

332
00:19:12,895 --> 00:19:14,915
computers, is not insubstantial.

333
00:19:15,215 --> 00:19:18,373
On the cost: nor should some kind of hearing prove burdensome.

334
00:19:18,673 --> 00:19:23,214
The opportunity for a meeting with a responsible employee empowered to resolve the

335
00:19:23,214 --> 00:19:27,589
dispute could be afforded well in advance of the scheduled date of termination.

336
00:19:27,889 --> 00:19:30,118
The utility had one argument left.

337
00:19:30,418 --> 00:19:35,268
Petitioners contend that the available common-law remedies of a pretermination

338
00:19:35,268 --> 00:19:40,118
injunction, a post-termination suit for damages, and post-payment action for a

339
00:19:40,118 --> 00:19:44,907
refund are sufficient to cure any perceived inadequacy in MLG&W's procedures.

340
00:19:45,207 --> 00:19:46,740
Why not just sue afterwards?

341
00:19:47,040 --> 00:19:51,489
The Court noted it had been advanced only obliquely below, and answered in three

342
00:19:51,489 --> 00:19:51,823
moves.

343
00:19:52,123 --> 00:19:52,959
First.

344
00:19:53,259 --> 00:19:58,440
Although utility service may be restored ultimately, the cessation of essential

345
00:19:58,440 --> 00:20:02,965
services for any appreciable time works a uniquely final deprivation.

346
00:20:03,265 --> 00:20:04,147
Second.

347
00:20:04,447 --> 00:20:08,262
Moreover, the probability of error in utility cutoff decisions is not so

348
00:20:08,262 --> 00:20:12,342
insubstantial as to warrant dispensing with all process prior to termination.

349
00:20:12,642 --> 00:20:13,524
Third.

350
00:20:13,824 --> 00:20:18,546
Equitable remedies are particularly unsuited to the resolution of factual disputes

351
00:20:18,546 --> 00:20:23,325
typically involving sums of money too small to justify engaging counsel or bringing

352
00:20:23,325 --> 00:20:23,901
a lawsuit.

353
00:20:24,201 --> 00:20:28,899
And to the suggestion that a customer can simply pay and claim it back later, the

354
00:20:28,899 --> 00:20:33,481
Court answered in a footnote that this ignores the predicament confronting many

355
00:20:33,481 --> 00:20:38,179
individuals who lack the means to pay additional, unanticipated utility expenses.

356
00:20:38,479 --> 00:20:43,645
Even under MLG&W's admirable credit procedures, the customer must make immediate

357
00:20:43,645 --> 00:20:46,745
payment of one-half of a disputed past due bill.

358
00:20:47,045 --> 00:20:48,670
So, the holding.

359
00:20:48,970 --> 00:20:53,689
Because of the failure to provide notice reasonably calculated to apprise

360
00:20:53,689 --> 00:20:58,860
respondents of the availability of an administrative procedure to consider their

361
00:20:58,860 --> 00:21:04,031
complaint of erroneous billing, and the failure to afford them an opportunity to

362
00:21:04,031 --> 00:21:09,396
present their complaint to a designated employee empowered to review disputed bills

363
00:21:09,396 --> 00:21:14,438
and rectify error, petitioners deprived respondents of an interest in property

364
00:21:14,438 --> 00:21:16,184
without due process of law.

365
00:21:16,484 --> 00:21:19,038
The judgment of the Court of Appeals is affirmed.

366
00:21:19,338 --> 00:21:23,425
And then, inside the reasoning that got there, one more sentence.

367
00:21:25,225 --> 00:21:29,657
And petitioners would retain the option to terminate service after affording this

368
00:21:29,657 --> 00:21:33,213
opportunity and concluding that the amount billed was justly due.

369
00:21:33,813 --> 00:21:35,624
Retain the option to terminate.

370
00:21:35,924 --> 00:21:37,782
After affording the opportunity.

371
00:21:38,082 --> 00:21:40,404
After concluding the amount was justly due.

372
00:21:40,704 --> 00:21:42,097
The meter reader stays.

373
00:21:42,397 --> 00:21:44,719
The four-day final notice stays.

374
00:21:45,019 --> 00:21:46,691
The thirty-day clock stays.

375
00:21:46,991 --> 00:21:51,912
What is added is three items on the page, and a designated employee obliged to hear

376
00:21:51,912 --> 00:21:52,564
the answer.

377
00:21:52,864 --> 00:21:55,558
Then the list of things the Court did not decide.

378
00:21:55,858 --> 00:21:58,087
It ordered nobody's service restored.

379
00:21:58,387 --> 00:21:59,827
It enjoined nothing.

380
00:22:00,127 --> 00:22:01,520
It awarded no money.

381
00:22:01,820 --> 00:22:04,421
The damages claim went back down undecided.

382
00:22:04,721 --> 00:22:06,486
It did not revive the class.

383
00:22:06,786 --> 00:22:09,108
The Crafts had asked for more than they got.

384
00:22:09,408 --> 00:22:11,219
An impartial decision maker.

385
00:22:11,519 --> 00:22:15,374
The opportunity to present information and rebut the records presented.

386
00:22:15,674 --> 00:22:16,835
A written decision.

387
00:22:17,135 --> 00:22:22,017
As respondents have not cross-petitioned, the Court wrote, we do not decide whether,

388
00:22:22,017 --> 00:22:26,841
or under what circumstances, any of these additional procedures may be appropriate.

389
00:22:27,141 --> 00:22:30,113
On the shape of the meeting the Court was equally reluctant.

390
00:22:30,413 --> 00:22:34,884
The public utility enjoys a broad discretion in the scheduling and structuring of

391
00:22:34,884 --> 00:22:39,300
this hearing, provided that the customer is afforded adequate time for effective

392
00:22:39,300 --> 00:22:42,116
presentation of his complaint prior to termination.

393
00:22:42,416 --> 00:22:43,577
Broad discretion.

394
00:22:43,877 --> 00:22:48,627
The resolution of a disputed bill normally presents a limited factual issue

395
00:22:48,627 --> 00:22:50,843
susceptible of informal resolution.

396
00:22:51,143 --> 00:22:53,697
The utility had already rewritten its notice.

397
00:22:53,997 --> 00:22:58,452
Petitioners have moved to clarify and regularize their notice procedure, and it is

398
00:22:58,452 --> 00:23:02,635
possible that the revised notice presently afforded may be entirely adequate.

399
00:23:02,935 --> 00:23:03,864
Possible.

400
00:23:04,164 --> 00:23:05,046
Not held.

401
00:23:05,346 --> 00:23:07,111
Now the dissent in full.

402
00:23:07,411 --> 00:23:11,451
Justice Stevens, with the Chief Justice and Justice Rehnquist, dissenting.

403
00:23:11,751 --> 00:23:16,614
In my judgment, the Court's holding confuses and trivializes the principle that the

404
00:23:16,614 --> 00:23:21,418
State may not deprive any person of life, liberty, or property without due process

405
00:23:21,418 --> 00:23:21,828
of law.

406
00:23:22,128 --> 00:23:24,125
He gave up the property point at once.

407
00:23:24,425 --> 00:23:29,203
I have no quarrel with the Court's conclusion that as a matter of Tennessee law a

408
00:23:29,203 --> 00:23:34,158
customer has a legitimate claim of entitlement to continued utility services as long

409
00:23:34,158 --> 00:23:37,521
as the undisputed portions of his utility bills are paid.

410
00:23:37,821 --> 00:23:39,493
His fight was about the record.

411
00:23:39,793 --> 00:23:44,561
The number on the notice, and the thirty or forty people who answered it, are in the

412
00:23:44,561 --> 00:23:45,923
dissent's opening pages.

413
00:23:46,223 --> 00:23:49,938
And his account of the dispute itself reverses the majority's.

414
00:23:50,238 --> 00:23:54,787
The Crafts, believing they were being billed twice for the same utilities, did not

415
00:23:54,787 --> 00:23:56,229
pay on the second account.

416
00:23:56,529 --> 00:24:00,954
In fact, the two accounts were independent; because the Crafts refused to pay the

417
00:24:00,954 --> 00:24:05,270
balance on the second account, the Division terminated their service on several

418
00:24:05,270 --> 00:24:05,817
occasions.

419
00:24:06,117 --> 00:24:10,465
The District Court expressly found that the Division sent a final notice before each

420
00:24:10,465 --> 00:24:11,086
termination.

421
00:24:11,386 --> 00:24:13,615
Five times, said the majority.

422
00:24:13,915 --> 00:24:15,819
Several occasions, said the dissent.

423
00:24:16,119 --> 00:24:20,623
The trial judge wrote that when she called after that final notice, she was given no

424
00:24:20,623 --> 00:24:21,320
satisfaction.

425
00:24:21,620 --> 00:24:23,431
The dissent read the same record.

426
00:24:23,731 --> 00:24:28,179
She was successful in working out a deferred-payment arrangement but apparently was

427
00:24:28,179 --> 00:24:30,697
unable to have the amount of the bills reduced.

428
00:24:30,997 --> 00:24:35,523
The record therefore indicates that Mrs. Craft did meet with Division employees

429
00:24:35,523 --> 00:24:40,221
having adequate authority but simply failed to persuade any of them that there was

430
00:24:40,221 --> 00:24:41,539
any error in her bills.

431
00:24:41,839 --> 00:24:46,345
Mrs. Craft testified on direct examination that after being cut off she went to the

432
00:24:46,345 --> 00:24:49,873
Division's office with the record of her payments on one account.

433
00:24:50,173 --> 00:24:52,959
She was told that she had to pay on the other account as well.

434
00:24:53,259 --> 00:24:57,713
In other words, an official of the Division did resolve the Crafts' dispute,

435
00:24:57,713 --> 00:24:59,296
correctly as it turned out.

436
00:24:59,596 --> 00:25:00,757
Same sentence.

437
00:25:01,057 --> 00:25:02,079
Same office.

438
00:25:02,379 --> 00:25:03,679
Which of them was right?

439
00:25:03,979 --> 00:25:06,347
The majority answered that in a single line.

440
00:25:06,647 --> 00:25:11,338
The dissent advances its own reading of the record in this case, but offers no

441
00:25:11,338 --> 00:25:15,006
justification for sidestepping the determinations made below.

442
00:25:15,306 --> 00:25:16,839
The dissent kept going.

443
00:25:17,139 --> 00:25:21,623
The Division's procedures would not be unconstitutional even if we assumed that

444
00:25:21,623 --> 00:25:25,823
Division employees, like federal judges, are occasionally discourteous and

445
00:25:25,823 --> 00:25:27,356
occasionally make mistakes.

446
00:25:27,656 --> 00:25:32,162
The Due Process Clause does not guarantee a correct or a courteous resolution of

447
00:25:32,162 --> 00:25:32,950
every dispute.

448
00:25:33,250 --> 00:25:34,968
Then his central objection.

449
00:25:35,268 --> 00:25:40,171
For a homeowner surely need not be told how to complain about an error in a utility

450
00:25:40,171 --> 00:25:45,015
bill; it is, of course, helpful to include the telephone number and office address

451
00:25:45,015 --> 00:25:49,977
in the termination notice, but our democratic government would cease to function if,

452
00:25:49,977 --> 00:25:54,880
as the Court seems to assume, our citizenry were unable to find such information on

453
00:25:54,880 --> 00:25:56,120
their own initiative.

454
00:25:56,420 --> 00:26:00,883
The Court's holding that the Division's notice was constitutionally defective rests

455
00:26:00,883 --> 00:26:03,572
on a paternalistic predicate that I cannot accept.

456
00:26:03,872 --> 00:26:05,265
And then the reductio.

457
00:26:05,565 --> 00:26:10,243
Even accepting the Court's predicate, a notice which advises customers to call the

458
00:26:10,243 --> 00:26:14,522
information center should be adequate; if not, it seems clear that advising

459
00:26:14,522 --> 00:26:19,257
customers to call, during normal business hours, a dispute resolution center manned

460
00:26:19,257 --> 00:26:22,794
by the same personnel would cure the constitutional objection.

461
00:26:23,094 --> 00:26:27,674
Distinctions of this small magnitude are the appropriate concern of administrative

462
00:26:27,674 --> 00:26:31,360
rulemaking; they are too trivial to identify constitutional error.

463
00:26:31,660 --> 00:26:32,682
Same building.

464
00:26:32,982 --> 00:26:34,050
Same staff.

465
00:26:34,350 --> 00:26:36,022
A different sign on the door.

466
00:26:36,322 --> 00:26:40,455
The majority had already answered the predicate, in a footnote of its own.

467
00:26:40,755 --> 00:26:44,830
In a different context a person threatened with the deprivation of a protected

468
00:26:44,830 --> 00:26:47,024
interest need not be told how to complain.

469
00:26:47,324 --> 00:26:52,202
But the prior decisions of this Court make clear that due process is flexible and

470
00:26:52,202 --> 00:26:56,658
calls for such procedural protections as the particular situation demands.

471
00:26:56,958 --> 00:27:01,580
Here, however, the notice is given to thousands of customers of various levels of

472
00:27:01,580 --> 00:27:03,692
education, experience, and resources.

473
00:27:03,992 --> 00:27:08,073
Lay consumers of electric service, the uninterrupted continuity of which is

474
00:27:08,073 --> 00:27:12,644
essential to health and safety, should be informed clearly of the availability of an

475
00:27:12,644 --> 00:27:14,766
opportunity to present their complaint.

476
00:27:15,066 --> 00:27:19,814
Although the Division's terminations number about 2,000 each month, the record does

477
00:27:19,814 --> 00:27:22,961
not reveal any actual case of harm to health or safety.

478
00:27:23,261 --> 00:27:27,586
The District Court found that the Division does not discontinue service when there

479
00:27:27,586 --> 00:27:28,694
is illness in a home.

480
00:27:28,994 --> 00:27:30,201
And then the dilemma.

481
00:27:30,501 --> 00:27:34,914
A potential loss of utility service sufficiently grievous to qualify as a

482
00:27:34,914 --> 00:27:39,810
constitutional deprivation can hardly be too petty to justify invoking the aid of

483
00:27:39,810 --> 00:27:41,321
counsel or the judiciary.

484
00:27:41,621 --> 00:27:46,292
Conversely, routine billing disputes too petty for the bench or the bar can hardly

485
00:27:46,292 --> 00:27:48,912
merit extraordinary constitutional protection.

486
00:27:49,212 --> 00:27:50,141
Which is it?

487
00:27:50,441 --> 00:27:53,088
That is the dissent's question to the majority.

488
00:27:53,388 --> 00:27:58,279
Against the majority's picture of a household priced out of a lawyer, he noted that

489
00:27:58,279 --> 00:28:02,994
the Crafts' dispute involved only a relatively small amount, but they did obtain

490
00:28:02,994 --> 00:28:06,530
counsel and thereafter they encountered no billing problems.

491
00:28:06,830 --> 00:28:08,223
Then the charge itself.

492
00:28:08,523 --> 00:28:13,506
These justifications suggest that the Court's new rule is the product of a policy

493
00:28:13,506 --> 00:28:17,997
determination rather than a traditional construction of the Constitution.

494
00:28:18,297 --> 00:28:22,986
As judges we have experience in appraising the fairness of legal remedies and

495
00:28:22,986 --> 00:28:27,554
judicial proceedings, but we have no similar ability to balance the cost of

496
00:28:27,554 --> 00:28:32,182
scheduling thousands of billing conferences against the benefit of providing

497
00:28:32,182 --> 00:28:37,176
additional protection to the occasional customer who may be unable to forestall an

498
00:28:37,176 --> 00:28:38,638
unjustified termination.

499
00:28:38,938 --> 00:28:40,145
Then his closing.

500
00:28:40,445 --> 00:28:45,741
It is an unfortunate fact that when the State assesses taxes or operates a utility,

501
00:28:45,741 --> 00:28:48,293
it occasionally overcharges the citizen.

502
00:28:48,593 --> 00:28:53,498
But if the State has given the citizen fair notice and afforded him procedural

503
00:28:53,498 --> 00:28:58,592
redress which is entirely adequate when invoked by his lawyer, the demands of the

504
00:28:58,592 --> 00:29:00,667
Due Process Clause are satisfied.

505
00:29:00,967 --> 00:29:05,453
I do not believe the Constitution requires the State to employ procedures that are

506
00:29:05,453 --> 00:29:10,049
so simple that every lay person can always act effectively without the assistance of

507
00:29:10,049 --> 00:29:10,487
counsel.

508
00:29:10,787 --> 00:29:12,227
I respectfully dissent.

509
00:29:12,527 --> 00:29:15,917
That is where the two opinions stop describing the same woman.

510
00:29:17,717 --> 00:29:22,020
Two bills came to one house, and the second was addressed in a name one letter

511
00:29:22,020 --> 00:29:24,172
different from the man who lived there.

512
00:29:24,472 --> 00:29:26,887
The record never settled who put it there.

513
00:29:27,187 --> 00:29:31,838
A footnote quoting the Crafts' own brief says the utility failed to combine the two

514
00:29:31,838 --> 00:29:32,342
accounts.

515
00:29:32,642 --> 00:29:37,186
The body of the opinion says a contractor the Crafts hired failed to consolidate the

516
00:29:37,186 --> 00:29:37,565
meters.

517
00:29:37,865 --> 00:29:42,091
Both sentences are printed in the same opinion, and the Court chose neither.

518
00:29:42,391 --> 00:29:46,060
Pay on the other bill as well, an employee of uncertain authority told her.

519
00:29:46,360 --> 00:29:51,387
The majority prints that as a brush-off, made without explanation and without any

520
00:29:51,387 --> 00:29:52,815
attempt to investigate.

521
00:29:53,115 --> 00:29:57,624
The dissent prints it as an official of the Division resolving a dispute, correctly

522
00:29:57,624 --> 00:29:58,548
as it turned out.

523
00:29:58,848 --> 00:30:03,446
The majority counted five terminations; the dissent called them several occasions.

524
00:30:03,746 --> 00:30:07,601
The opinion gives no month, no duration and no figure for any of them.

525
00:30:07,901 --> 00:30:09,805
Nor did anyone settle the money.

526
00:30:10,105 --> 00:30:12,938
Possible gas overcharges, said the trial court.

527
00:30:13,238 --> 00:30:16,024
Genuinely independent accounts, said the dissent.

528
00:30:16,324 --> 00:30:20,039
The Supreme Court expressed no opinion and sent the question back down.

529
00:30:20,339 --> 00:30:22,150
No damages were awarded here.

530
00:30:22,450 --> 00:30:24,633
No court found that the bill was wrong.

531
00:30:24,933 --> 00:30:29,438
Behind the notice there was a procedure, as the District Court described it.

532
00:30:29,738 --> 00:30:34,289
Credit counselors, the chief clerk, the supervisor, the Board of Commissioners.

533
00:30:34,589 --> 00:30:37,700
Thirty-three thousand complaints about high bills in one year.

534
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:42,563
A telephone number printed on the notice, answered, in the dissent's account, by

535
00:30:42,563 --> 00:30:46,499
thirty or forty people who could each delay the cutoff by three days.

536
00:30:46,799 --> 00:30:51,398
No description of any of it was ever distributed to customers, and the record does

537
00:30:51,398 --> 00:30:54,090
not establish which flyer reached Alaska Street.

538
00:30:54,390 --> 00:30:57,780
What the case added is three items and one obligation.

539
00:30:58,080 --> 00:30:59,520
Where.

540
00:30:59,820 --> 00:31:00,749
What hours.

541
00:31:01,049 --> 00:31:02,071
Before whom.

542
00:31:02,371 --> 00:31:06,989
And a designated employee with the power to correct the bill, obliged to hear the

543
00:31:06,989 --> 00:31:09,383
complaint before the meter reader arrives.

544
00:31:09,683 --> 00:31:12,284
And after that, the meter reader may still arrive.

545
00:31:12,584 --> 00:31:17,246
The option to terminate survives the hearing: hear the complaint, then decide the

546
00:31:17,246 --> 00:31:19,318
money is owed, then cut the service.

547
00:31:19,618 --> 00:31:21,151
Bill at about day four.

548
00:31:21,451 --> 00:31:23,355
Net due at about day twenty.

549
00:31:23,655 --> 00:31:26,906
Final notice at about day twenty-four, giving four days.

550
00:31:27,206 --> 00:31:28,367
Then the meter reader.

551
00:31:28,667 --> 00:31:31,268
About five days after that, everything else.

552
00:31:31,568 --> 00:31:36,215
On one occasion, after a final notice, a woman in Memphis telephoned the offices of

553
00:31:36,215 --> 00:31:39,463
her city's utility and explained that she had paid a bill.

554
00:31:39,763 --> 00:31:44,638
Three courts, four years, and a record that never agreed with itself — all out of

555
00:31:44,638 --> 00:31:47,286
what happened at the other end of that line.

556
00:31:47,586 --> 00:31:49,583
She was given no satisfaction.
